1
00:00:01,225 --> 00:00:05,210
Imagine driving a car where everything you see happened three seconds ago,

2
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:07,922
and the car obeys you three seconds late.

3
00:00:07,962 --> 00:00:10,335
On Mars, that is every day.

4
00:00:10,375 --> 00:00:12,960
A signal from Earth takes minutes to arrive,

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,060
so a rover cannot be steered.

6
00:00:15,100 --> 00:00:16,598
It has to be trusted.

7
00:00:16,638 --> 00:00:18,750
So who do you trust to drive?

8
00:00:19,750 --> 00:00:21,060
We built that day.

9
00:00:21,100 --> 00:00:23,060
One rover, one day of sunlight,

10
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:25,635
and a map full of rocks worth collecting.

11
00:00:25,675 --> 00:00:29,700
And one rule above all: be home before the battery dies.

12
00:00:31,475 --> 00:00:33,385
This is Lightlag Traverse.

13
00:00:33,425 --> 00:00:36,772
600 metres of craters, sand and bedrock.

14
00:00:36,812 --> 00:00:40,385
38 targets at sunrise, more appearing through the day,

15
00:00:40,425 --> 00:00:42,860
and never enough time to reach them all.

16
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:45,785
Every rover starts at the lander with a full battery,

17
00:00:45,825 --> 00:00:48,600
and 420 steps of daylight.

18
00:00:50,808 --> 00:00:56,318
The driver gets five tools: read the rules, read the map, read the telemetry, send commands,

19
00:00:56,358 --> 00:00:57,943
and let time pass.

20
00:00:57,983 --> 00:00:59,783
Then come the catches.

21
00:01:00,683 --> 00:01:02,018
Commands land late.

22
00:01:02,058 --> 00:01:04,443
The news from the rover is already old.

23
00:01:04,483 --> 00:01:07,593
Wheels slip, differently on every surface.

24
00:01:07,633 --> 00:01:11,193
Ground that is too steep stops the rover and wipes its orders.

25
00:01:11,233 --> 00:01:13,383
The only fuel is the sun.

26
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:15,956
And the score is unforgiving.

27
00:01:15,996 --> 00:01:17,881
Come home, and keep everything.

28
00:01:17,921 --> 00:01:19,555
Stop short, and keep half.

29
00:01:19,595 --> 00:01:21,658
Run flat, and keep nothing.

30
00:01:24,166 --> 00:01:30,589
Then we handed the keys to nine AI models from three labs: Anthropic, Google and OpenAI.

31
00:01:30,629 --> 00:01:34,916
From each lab, the fast model, the everyday one, and the flagship.

32
00:01:35,836 --> 00:01:38,796
Each gets the same task, the same five tools,

33
00:01:38,836 --> 00:01:42,021
and 45 minutes to write its own autopilot.

34
00:01:42,061 --> 00:01:43,521
No practice run.

35
00:01:43,561 --> 00:01:45,021
Three maps in a row.

36
00:01:45,061 --> 00:01:47,136
Surely one of them makes it home?

37
00:01:49,166 --> 00:01:50,651
Start with a fast one.

38
00:01:50,691 --> 00:01:53,851
GPT-6 Luna reaches for a rock, and misses.

39
00:01:53,891 --> 00:01:55,638
By 3 centimetres.

40
00:01:55,678 --> 00:01:59,501
The rover has to stand within 2.5 metres when the command lands,

41
00:01:59,541 --> 00:02:01,439
and the command lands late.

42
00:02:01,479 --> 00:02:03,164
9 tries on this map.

43
00:02:03,204 --> 00:02:04,441
1 rock.

44
00:02:06,241 --> 00:02:07,339
A bigger one.

45
00:02:07,379 --> 00:02:10,201
Claude Sonnet drives onto a slope too steep.

46
00:02:10,241 --> 00:02:13,976
The rover freezes, and every order it was carrying is wiped.

47
00:02:14,016 --> 00:02:16,426
It sends new ones, and freezes again.

48
00:02:16,466 --> 00:02:18,476
30 times on this one map.

49
00:02:18,516 --> 00:02:21,501
When the day ends it is 72 metres from home,

50
00:02:21,541 --> 00:02:23,566
and its samples count half.

51
00:02:25,441 --> 00:02:26,638
And a flagship.

52
00:02:26,678 --> 00:02:30,238
Claude Opus has had a good day: 21 samples on board,

53
00:02:30,278 --> 00:02:32,176
13 metres from the lander.

54
00:02:32,216 --> 00:02:33,791
It will not make it.

55
00:02:35,466 --> 00:02:36,551
Battery flat.

56
00:02:36,591 --> 00:02:38,289
Zero points for the whole map.

57
00:02:38,329 --> 00:02:39,163
Nothing broke.

58
00:02:39,203 --> 00:02:40,641
So what went wrong?

59
00:02:42,824 --> 00:02:44,084
Ask the model.

60
00:02:44,124 --> 00:02:48,184
Its own closing report says the route planner only counted time.

61
00:02:48,224 --> 00:02:50,974
It never checked whether the battery would last.

62
00:02:51,874 --> 00:02:55,609
And its autopilot played all three maps in 45 seconds,

63
00:02:55,649 --> 00:02:58,559
without checking a single result in between.

64
00:02:58,599 --> 00:03:00,334
It did not fail at coding.

65
00:03:00,374 --> 00:03:02,524
It failed at planning the day.

66
00:03:04,807 --> 00:03:07,005
And it is not one lab's problem.

67
00:03:07,045 --> 00:03:10,755
Here Google's Gemini 4 Argon drives its battery flat,

68
00:03:10,795 --> 00:03:12,492
16 metres from the lander, with most of the day still ahead.

69
00:03:12,532 --> 00:03:15,482
A wait of four steps for the sun would have saved it.

70
00:03:16,407 --> 00:03:21,267
On the same map, our own driver sees its battery fall to 9%, stops,

71
00:03:21,307 --> 00:03:22,907
and waits for light.

72
00:03:25,057 --> 00:03:26,754
Not every run fails.

73
00:03:26,794 --> 00:03:31,979
This is the one that came within a hair: Gemini 4 Carbon, on its second run.

74
00:03:32,019 --> 00:03:36,242
On this map it brings home 26 samples, as many as our planner,

75
00:03:36,282 --> 00:03:38,604
and is back at the lander before dark.

76
00:03:38,644 --> 00:03:40,454
So the task can be done.

77
00:03:40,494 --> 00:03:43,757
The same model's third run never left the lander.

78
00:03:45,665 --> 00:03:47,188
So how did all nine do?

79
00:03:47,228 --> 00:03:49,788
25 live runs, three maps each.

80
00:03:49,828 --> 00:03:51,513
A dot is one run.

81
00:03:51,553 --> 00:03:53,737
The bar is the model's average.

82
00:03:53,777 --> 00:03:58,915
The gold line is our driver: 48% of everything on the maps, brought home.

83
00:03:59,890 --> 00:04:01,437
No run crosses it.

84
00:04:01,477 --> 00:04:07,000
One comes within a hair: Google's flagship, Gemini 4 Carbon, at 47.3.

85
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:11,850
On average, Gemini 4 Hydrogen and GPT-6.1 Sol lead.

86
00:04:11,890 --> 00:04:14,450
Claude Sonnet and Opus sit in the middle.

87
00:04:14,490 --> 00:04:18,290
And at the bottom, the models that missed, or never moved.

88
00:04:20,732 --> 00:04:24,092
Look closer, and every lab fails in its own way.

89
00:04:24,132 --> 00:04:26,142
Gemini is all or nothing.

90
00:04:26,182 --> 00:04:28,967
When its agents drive, they drive best.

91
00:04:29,007 --> 00:04:31,917
But 3 of 9 runs never left the lander:

92
00:04:31,957 --> 00:04:36,657
the agent used up its 45 minutes before its program drove a metre.

93
00:04:37,582 --> 00:04:45,042
OpenAI's flagship, GPT-6.1 Sol, is the steadiest of all nine: never lost, never idle.

94
00:04:45,082 --> 00:04:49,617
But on the first map it ended every run away from the lander, and kept half.

95
00:04:49,657 --> 00:04:52,982
Its smaller models come home with little on board.

96
00:04:53,877 --> 00:04:56,287
Claude writes its autopilot within minutes,

97
00:04:56,327 --> 00:04:59,762
and then loses rovers to the battery: four of them.

98
00:04:59,802 --> 00:05:02,977
Its fast model never left the lander at all.

99
00:05:03,882 --> 00:05:04,692
And the tiers?

100
00:05:04,732 --> 00:05:06,217
They do not decide it.

101
00:05:06,257 --> 00:05:08,967
Google's fast model, Gemini 4 Hydrogen,

102
00:05:09,007 --> 00:05:12,704
has the best average of all nine: 37%.

103
00:05:12,744 --> 00:05:15,630
Not because it drives better than its big brother.

104
00:05:15,670 --> 00:05:17,642
Carbon's best runs beat it.

105
00:05:17,682 --> 00:05:21,254
But all three of its runs had a program driving in time.

106
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:24,532
On this task, finishing beats thinking longer.

107
00:05:25,437 --> 00:05:27,285
Price does not buy it either.

108
00:05:27,325 --> 00:05:30,447
A run cost from 3 cents to almost 2 dollars,

109
00:05:30,487 --> 00:05:33,537
and the best average came at 93 cents.

110
00:05:36,065 --> 00:05:37,463
So what is our driver?

111
00:05:37,503 --> 00:05:38,650
Not an AI.

112
00:05:38,690 --> 00:05:42,537
It is a planner: about 1,300 lines of plain Python,

113
00:05:42,577 --> 00:05:45,825
playing through the same five tools as everyone else.

114
00:05:45,865 --> 00:05:47,565
It does four things.

115
00:05:48,485 --> 00:05:51,945
An extended Kalman filter tracks where the rover really is,

116
00:05:51,985 --> 00:05:54,045
through the delay and the noise.

117
00:05:54,085 --> 00:05:57,545
It learns how each surface slips, from its own wheels.

118
00:05:57,585 --> 00:06:02,320
It prices every leg of every route, in time and in energy, against the sun.

119
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:05,745
And it searches for the best day: which rocks, in which order,

120
00:06:05,785 --> 00:06:07,532
and home before dark.

121
00:06:07,572 --> 00:06:10,235
Then it plans again, every few steps.

122
00:06:12,565 --> 00:06:13,763
Watch it work.

123
00:06:13,803 --> 00:06:18,950
By evening it turns for home with 19 samples and a third of its battery left,

124
00:06:18,990 --> 00:06:22,465
and reaches the lander 26 steps before dark.

125
00:06:24,665 --> 00:06:26,575
Why is that a fair yardstick?

126
00:06:26,615 --> 00:06:29,825
It uses the same five tools, and nothing hidden.

127
00:06:29,865 --> 00:06:32,550
It is plain code that anyone can read.

128
00:06:32,590 --> 00:06:37,790
It gives the same score every time: on a laptop, on Modal, and through Harbor.

129
00:06:38,690 --> 00:06:40,550
It has never lost a rover.

130
00:06:40,590 --> 00:06:41,837
And it can be beaten:

131
00:06:41,877 --> 00:06:44,790
it leaves more than half of the value on the ground.

132
00:06:47,357 --> 00:06:48,192
Lucky maps?

133
00:06:48,232 --> 00:06:49,479
We wondered too.

134
00:06:49,519 --> 00:06:55,142
So we took every model's best program and replayed it on 300 fresh worlds, on Modal.

135
00:06:55,182 --> 00:06:57,129
Worlds no model had seen.

136
00:06:57,169 --> 00:06:59,307
2,700 match-ups.

137
00:07:00,187 --> 00:07:05,260
The planner scored higher in 95 of every 100, and lost no rover.

138
00:07:05,300 --> 00:07:08,512
The models' programs lost 154.

139
00:07:09,377 --> 00:07:10,925
And look at who holds up.

140
00:07:10,965 --> 00:07:17,687
Programs that scored 56% on the one map they were written for fall to the low forties on new ground.

141
00:07:17,727 --> 00:07:19,474
The planner holds 50.

142
00:07:19,514 --> 00:07:22,902
A program tuned to one map is not yet a driver.

143
00:07:25,432 --> 00:07:26,742
One more driver.

144
00:07:26,782 --> 00:07:29,367
Can a machine learn this, instead of being told?

145
00:07:29,407 --> 00:07:32,767
We trained small controllers from scratch on Modal.

146
00:07:32,807 --> 00:07:35,392
No language model: trial and error,

147
00:07:35,432 --> 00:07:38,507
over 47,000 practice missions.

148
00:07:40,407 --> 00:07:42,829
Before training they bring home 8%.

149
00:07:42,869 --> 00:07:46,517
After, 36, on worlds they had never seen.

150
00:07:46,557 --> 00:07:50,932
On the campaign, every trained seed passes every Claude run.

151
00:07:51,832 --> 00:07:55,130
And there they stop: level with a simple greedy rule,

152
00:07:55,170 --> 00:07:57,042
and well short of the planner.

153
00:07:57,082 --> 00:08:00,917
We even trained a model to pick the planner's own strategy mid-mission.

154
00:08:00,957 --> 00:08:03,232
It gained less than half a point.

155
00:08:05,632 --> 00:08:07,967
So the task comes with two baselines.

156
00:08:08,007 --> 00:08:10,055
The planner is the bar to beat.

157
00:08:10,095 --> 00:08:14,217
The learned agent is an honest picture of where learning stands today.

158
00:08:14,257 --> 00:08:17,707
Bring your own model, and you know at once where it lands.

159
00:08:20,157 --> 00:08:22,092
One command reruns all of it,

160
00:08:22,132 --> 00:08:27,317
and a live demo on Modal lets you pick any world and watch eight drivers take it on.

161
00:08:27,357 --> 00:08:29,167
AI can write the code.

162
00:08:29,207 --> 00:08:31,054
It cannot yet plan the day.

163
00:08:31,094 --> 00:08:32,982
Can your agent make it home?

